SM(566,{"n":"2 Corinthians 7","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/2 Corinthians 7.mp3","d":2398.7,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.28,"Okay, so we'll go ahead and get started."],[22.72,"When we get started, we're gonna be picking up uh at the beginning of uh St."],[27.52,"Paul's Second Epistle to the Corinthians, uh, chapter seven."],[31.92,"And to get us a little caught up, which is important tonight, because even though we're picking up at a chapter break, that wasn't really much of a break in the text."],[40.64,"It was just the chapter."],[42.56,"As we've mentioned before, these chapter and verse breaks were put in many, many, many centuries after these texts were written."],[51.2,"And so they, in some cases, the chapter divisions are primarily based on the lectionary, like the reading cycle."],[59.36,"So there're just sort of chunks of a certain length in a lot of cases."],[63.48,"And so sometimes they're in sort of a good spot that makes sense, where there's sort of a break in theme, and then sometimes they had just gone a long time since they had one, and so they put one in."],[74.28,"And so it varies a little."],[76.12,"The same thing with the verse breaks."],[78.28,"They often, when they make English translations of the Bible, make it look like the verse breaks are just sort of each sentence, or they try to match it up that way, but that does not correspond to Greek sentences, like in any way at all."],[92.44,"So sometimes there's multiple Greek sentences in one English verse, and vice versa."],[97.56,"More often, Greek sentences are very long and complex, and they end up getting broken up into four or five English sentences."],[104.92,"That's because in Greek, Greek isn't as bad as Latin in this respect"],[109.92,"Latin, like in Julius Caesar's Conquest of Gaul, he has a sentence that goes on for 17 pages at one point."],[119.28,"And the last word is the last word is the subject."],[124.64,"But Greek isn't quite that bad."],[127.12,"But especially with St."],[128.56,"Paul, who has relatively sophisticated Greek for the New Testament, you can go on a good long time."],[135.92,"You can have a sentence that runs a page or two because Greek doesn't really have run-on sentences."],[141.52,"You can just keep adding clauses to your heart's content for as long as you want to."],[146.92,"And of course, even that being said, when these were written, as we've mentioned before, they were written in all capitals with no punctuation and no space between the words."],[157.48,"Just continuously. And no hyphens in the middle of words when they reach the end of the line."],[162.76,"They just continue."],[164.22,"On the next line."],[165.82,"So even, you know, those sentence breaks that you find in like a modern printed Greek version of the New Testament have been put there by somebody later and weren't put there by St."],[179.74,"Paul. So even that is kind of subjective."],[182.88,"So we don't actually know for sure when his sentences end."],[188.05,"Now the people who did the first manuscripts that had the punctuation in that kind of thing sent were centuries and centuries ago and were native Greek speakers, so they probably had a better idea than any of us, but I wouldn't stake my life and reputation on it."],[208.01,"And I think we've already seen, although it was back in the Gospels, that we saw my favorite example of this."],[213.37,"My favorite example of this is the verse from Isaiah that's quoted by at least all three of the Synoptic Gospels."],[219.21,"I don't remember off the top of my head whether Saint John quotes it too."],[221.77,"I don't think he does."],[223.69,"About the voice crying out in the wilderness."],[226.23,"And the way we're used to reading that in English, because this is the way it's been done in English since the King James Version, is a voice crying out in the wilderness, comma, quotation mark, makes straight the way of the Lord."],[236.95,"That's how we're used to reading it"],[242.81,"But again, that punctuation isn't there in any version of the Greek."],[246.81,"So it could just as easily be a voice crying out, comma, quotation mark, in the wilderness makes straight the way of the Lord."],[256.57,"Close quote. Oh."],[260.01,"That does change then."],[261.37,"And which it is, is a completely subjective judgment."],[267.55,"Pretty much. I mean, there's no real clue in the sense that there's no real clue in the text."],[273.56,"That would make it obvious which of those is intended."],[281.92,"Well, I argue for the second one based on the context in Isaiah."],[286.0,"But there are people who say that's based on my view of how New Testament authors quote the Old Testament."],[293.52,"I believe they quote the Old Testament, even though they're just quoting one verse, they're quoting it in context."],[298.48,"There are plenty of other people who argue, no, that the New Testament authors just take Old Testament verses and do something different with them."],[307.6,"So the Old Testament text, the Old Testament context doesn't apply then."],[311.76,"So if you take that view, then there's no way of knowing which it is in that Isaiah quote."],[316.4,"The only thing that could really tell you one way or the other is if you think the Old Testament context is important, you could make an argument one way or the other based on that."],[324.8,"that. But's about the only place to go"],[327.88,"So this is a little off topic, but relevant to all of that was an intro to why we need a little more introduction this time, since there's a chapter break here, but not but it's worth it's worth repeating because we're so used to reading the Bible with these chapter and verse breaks, and of thinking of the chapters as units."],[348.14,"As like chapters in a fiction book that we got today, or chapters in a non-fiction book that we got today, where the author designed it that way, that it bears repeating over and over again that that's not how St."],[360.38,"Paul wrote this letter."],[361.82,"He didn't have chapters."],[363.76,"He didn't have these sections."],[365.36,"This is sort of somebody's judgment for various reasons."],[369.04,"A lot of times liturg.ical reasons, like I mentioned in lectionaries"],[372.44,"Not even really textual interpretive reasons, but liturgical reasons that these things get separated."],[378.12,"So and and even the punctuation, even things like quotation marks and where they go."],[383.56,"There are places where that's debated."],[386.9,"So Saint Paul had just finished when we left off last time."],[391.7,"He had just finished uh quoting a series of of texts uh about holiness, about holiness in the sense of being separated, because he's still working hard."],[405.82,"And remember, his whole goal here in Second Corinthians is to try to get the community in Corinth peace back together and reconciled to each other."],[415.34,"After all the problems they had that he wrote about in 1 Corinthians, and then that has through his visit come to a head, and there's been this split."],[423.5,"There are people who are disciplined, who are put out of the congregation, who St."],[428.62,"Paul wants to see reconciled and reincorporated."],[432.4,"And so when we left off last time, he was kind of speaking to those people who had been kind of alienated or felt alienated or been excommunicated, essentially."],[442.89,"Either by the church, or they just felt that way."],[446.65,"Talking to them about coming back."],[449.07,"And he was talking about the community, the church community, the assembly."],[454.45,"The Christian assembly in Corinth, this community."],[458.01,"No man is an island."],[459.39,"So he was saying if you're not part of this community, if you stay outside, you're gonna have to join some other community."],[466.19,"Whether it's just the general pagan city community of Corinth, your trade guild that's worshiping other gods, as part of their trade guild activities, whatever it is, you're gonna end up in some community."],[476.38,"And he's and he was saying to them, look, you're Christians now."],[479.84,"You've been baptized into Christ, you've put on Christ, you've received the Holy Spirit."],[484.5,"You're a different person than you were when you were part of those communities."],[489.22,"And so you can't now go back and be part of one of those communities because you can't have fellowship with any of them anymore."],[497.88,"You've been changed, you've been transformed."],[499.72,"You don't have anything in common with those folks anymore"],[504.46,"And so you need to come back."],[506.55,"Come back and be restored."],[507.91,"Because this is the community where Christ is dwelling in your midst."],[511.91,"This is the community where salvation is happening."],[514.47,"This is the holy community."],[515.99,"This is where you belong now because of what Christ has done in your life."],[522.07,"And so you need to come back and be reconciled, despite the various difficulties that you might have about doing that."],[530.87,"And the humility that's going to require, the forgiveness that's going to require on all sides."],[536.31,"It has to happen."],[537.99,"He's arguing to them that it's just an imperative."],[542.63,"And so the last quote. he gave was God saying to Israel, He's actually put two quotes together"],[550.67,"But come out from among them and be separate, says the Lord."],[553.71,"Do not touch what is unclean, and I will receive you."],[556.35,"I will be a father to you, and you shall be my sons and daughters, says the Lord Almighty."],[562.03,"So there are those two pieces."],[564.43,"They need to separate themselves, and be a part of the holy community."],[568.67,"And that will have the result that then they'll be part of this community is God's family."],[575.23,"And that family structure, we've talked before about starting when we were going through Acts."],[581.33,"How in Acts it kept referring to the Christians in a city as the brethren."],[587.71,"Like the siblings, the brothers and sisters."],[590.56,"That family language was used there to describe members of Christian communities."],[594.4,"And we've seen St."],[595.28,"Paul using that language of brethren and that kind of thing to address the Christian people."],[600.64,"This family imagery isn't just a Christian thing."],[604.56,"This is community language in the first century AD."],[609.12,"So the whole society was built around these sort of family models, with in Roman terms the Potter Familius."],[616.98,"Is the head of the big extended family."],[619.94,"And that structure was then sort of reduplicated in cities and included the gods, of the city."],[627.58,"And then reduplicating the empire."],[629.88,"The emperor was the father of the empire."],[630.44,"He was like the potter familius of the whole body"],[636.72,"And so that's why Saint Paul moves back and forth so easily between this community and family length."],[642.24,"It's not just which community are you gonna be a part of, it's which family are you gonna be a part of."],[646.56,"And if you're a part of this family, if you're a part of the church family, then you have God as your father."],[652.0,"If you go and join one of those other families, you're gonna have somebody else."],[656.73,"You're gonna have somebody else who you don't want."],[659.18,"As your and again, we in our modern world have various images of fatherhood, a lot of that deeply influenced by Christianity."],[667.9,"Okay, but the the Potter Familius was had power within his family in in the the R Romomanan Empi Emrepi.re the way Caesar had power"],[678.74,"When someone in the family had a child, they would bring the child, they'd bring the baby to the Potter Familius."],[686.18,"He would inspect it, to make sure there were no birth defects or anything, and then decide whether he wanted to keep it or have them take it out and abandon it."],[695.96,"That's how it worked in the Roman Empire."],[698.28,"That's the level of authority that the Potter Familia, the father of the family, had."],[704.92,"Okay, it was literally the power of life and death, over the people in the family."],[709.66,"He was the master of all the slaves."],[711.84,"He was when it was when it said that he was the head of the household, it was like the emperor."],[716.96,"Could have people executed, could sell members of the family as slaves."],[723.69,"Could do whatever he wanted to."],[726.09,"So you have to understand that resonance of the imagery here."],[730.75,"When St. Paul presents it to them as choose, you can choose, you can be part of this family and have God as your father, knowing his character through Christ and what he's like, and have him have that kind of power over your life."],[746.67,"Or if you go and join one of these other families and have some pagan god or Caesar, or some triangular he'll have that kind of power in your life."],[760.85,"And how is how is he gonna use it right what's he what's he gonna do right when you make him your lord yeah yeah so that that resonance is there right this isn't just wouldn't it be nice to have God as your dad right that's not right that's that's who is gonna have this power of life and death over you who is the person who's going to be your benefactor and shape your destiny right and decide what your life is going to be like and so St."],[792.11,"Paul's presenting it that way because any sane person right would in the ancient world would choose God for obvious obvious reasons."],[804.07,"Yeah. Because they didn't view the the pagan gods same way."],[808.73,"Nobody's saying what a friend we have in Zeus."],[810.98,"This is why over and over again in our in our liturgy we talk about how God is a good God who loves mankind."],[817.7,"That our God loves human beings, which was a bizarre notion in the ancient world."],[824.82,"No one thought Zeus loved them."],[827.14,"They were worried Zeus might come and make love to them by force, but nobody thought Zeus loved them."],[834.18,"The gods would be sort of appeased and placated."],[838.87,"And manipulated, if possible, to do things you wanted to, but there was no like a relationship like this."],[846.99,"And that's why it's it we we it is.n't doesn't strike us as shocking anymore, so we just pass over it in the liturgy all the time"],[853.43,"Oh, yeah, God's a good God, he loves mankind."],[856.36,"Yeah, that's a big deal in the ancient world."],[861.72,"And so, yes, for the argument Saint Paul is making here to these Corinthians is an incredibly strong one at the time and in that place."],[871.65,"You have a God who's the creator of the universe who loves you and who wants to be your father in this world and in this life."],[879.17,"Or you can go back out there into the darkness."],[883.81,"Would you say we are the family of the following?"],[886.77,"A poter familius in Latin, which basically means the father of the family, but you say it in Latin, you sound smarter, so that's why I do that."],[893.57,"So, okay So this is, we're picking up sort of immediately after that quote, and St."],[904.09,"Paul is still in his in his discussion."],[907.99,"And he begins with in chapter 7, verse 1, therefore."],[912.63,"And we've talked about that, that St."],[914.23,"Paul uses that therefore."],[916.01,"That un in Greek as a transition."],[918.91,"So I've just made this argument, or I've just taught you this thing, or I've just explained this thing."],[924.35,"Now, here's what we need to do."],[926.53,"Now, here's how this applies to what you need to do."],[929.97,"Therefore, having these promises, beloved, let us cleanse ourselves from all filthiness of the flesh and spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of God."],[936.21,"Now that sounds like he's just sort of reiterating what he just said"],[943.66,"He's been talking about holiness anyway."],[946.3,"But holiness and purity and purification, we have to understand with nuance."],[957.28,"They aren't just synonyms."],[958.74,"They aren't just synonyms."],[960.02,"So we've talked about how holiness is being set apart."],[963.67,"So he's saying you need to perfect holiness in the fear of God."],[968.41,"That's our goal."],[969.61,"Is what you need to do."],[970.73,"Perfect holiness in the fear of God, meaning be truly separate."],[974.5,"Truly separate yourself from the world and the flesh."],[978.2,"And these things that have ensnared you before and are threatening to now ensnare you again since you're outside the church."],[984.16,"Fully separate yourselves."],[986.48,"How do you do that?"],[987.92,"How do you do that?"],[989.28,"So again, this is a concept that's kind of foreign to us in the modern world, but not foreign in the ancient world, especially to people who understood the Old Testament."],[1011.92,"But is about holiness."],[1013.94,"And sacred space and sacred things and sacred people."],[1018.1,"So there's it is one thing to set something apart and make it holy."],[1023.16,"So that's one thing."],[1024.7,"We do that."],[1025.5,"So, like I've said before, the difference between the chalice we use in liturgy and my Star Trek 2, The Wrath of Khan Collector's Cup, is not that the chalice we use in church in the liturgy is made of magical metal mined from a holy mountain somewhere."],[1045.54,"With special cut gems only found, you know, deep within Mount Athos or something."],[1051.64,"Like that's not that's not what makes it holy, and my cup not holy."],[1056.36,"What makes it holy is what it's used for."],[1059.8,"The chalice is set apart, and it's only used for one thing to hold the body and blood of Christ, and that's it."],[1066.52,"'s Whereas I drink all kinds of things out of my Star Trek 2, The Wrath of Khan Collector Cup."],[1072.8,"So that doesn't make it an evil cup, that just makes it a common thing."],[1078.1,"A common thing."],[1080.18,"You can take something that is holy and sacred that's been set apart like that, and you can desecrate it."],[1086.04,"Someone could break into the church and take that chalice, drink whiskey out of it."],[1091.61,"You know, throw it around."],[1093.22,"And it would no longer be holy and sacred anymore."],[1096.04,"Because it would have been used for common things."],[1098.92,"And this is this is important to understand when St."],[1102.04,"Paul and all the apostles talk about our own holiness."],[1104.78,"Because this is how they understand our own holiness."],[1107.9,"We, in our bapt.ism and in receiving the Holy Spirit, have been set apart, for holy purposes by God"],[1115.96,"And so he's constantly saying, like in 1 Corinthians, when he said, uh, your body is the temple of the Holy Spirit."],[1122.52,"You can't pollute it."],[1124.46,"If we take our body, we take our minds, we take our soul, and we go and we desecrate them, we just have to use them for common things."],[1134.23,"And now they're desecrated."],[1135.51,"Now we're not holy anymore."],[1137.11,"Now we're not set apart anymore."],[1139.27,"So if we're in that state, which is the state of the people Saint Paul is talking to, who he wants to perfect holiness, what do you need to do?"],[1149.03,"And he talks about cleansing ourselves from all filthiness of the flesh and spirit."],[1152.71,"So this is cleansing and purification language"],[1159.23,"So if someone broke into the church and used the chalice like that, drank liquor out of it, tossed it on the floor, what would we have to do if we wanted to use it as a chalice again?"],[1171.23,"We'd have to reconsecrate it."],[1173.15,"We'd have to cleanse it and purify it."],[1174.77,"We'd have to wash it."],[1176.55,"We'd have to cleanse it from all the stain and the damage."],[1180.49,"We might need to fix it up if it got broken when it got thrown on the floor."],[1184.27,"We need to restore it and clean it and purify it and then set it apart again."],[1189.0,"Set it apart again and reconsecrate it."],[1191.16,"So the same thing would be true of a space."],[1193.74,"The day of atonement every year."],[1196.71,"The when they took the second goat."],[1199.37,"There's the one goat who they put the sins of the people on, they send it out into the wilderness."],[1203.71,"To take the sins away."],[1205.31,"But then they take the other goat that's the goat for Yahweh, the God of Israel, that goat they sacrifice, and they take its blood, and they use it for what?"],[1215.15,"To make atonement for all the physical objects in the Holy of Holies."],[1221.87,"And that's the language it's used."],[1223.95,"They atone for the altar."],[1225.95,"They atone for the Ark of the Covenant."],[1228.35,"It's physical objects."],[1230.28,"Because what's been done in the camp in the wilderness, what's been done around the temple, in Jerusalem and in Israel."],[1237.92,"Has desecrated it."],[1239.9,"Has desecrated it."],[1241.26,"And so it needs to be purified and restored."],[1244.46,"So that it can continue to function as the place where God dwells."],[1247.98,"Just like we cleanse the chalice so it could continue to be the place that holds the body and blood of Christ."],[1254.72,"For God to stay in the tabernacle and continue to dwell there, it has to be purified from the stain, the residue."],[1261.2,"This is why you had to have the Day of Atonement once a year."],[1264.01,"Because you had sin offerings all the time."],[1265.69,"When people sinned, you did a sin offering."],[1267.85,"So then why do you need a day of atonement?"],[1269.93,"You need a day of atonement because you need to purify the sacred space and the sacred things from what's been done with them."],[1277.93,"Okay. So St."],[1280.09,"Paul is applying this to us."],[1283.03,"And to the Corinthians first, and then to us."],[1285.93,"These Corinthians were at one point baptized."],[1289.37,"They received the Holy Spirit."],[1291.47,"They received the Eucharist."],[1292.75,"They were part of the community."],[1294.37,"Where Christ is."],[1295.49,"They were set apart."],[1296.61,"They were holy."],[1297.73,"Remember, he called them saints."],[1299.33,"He called them holy ones in 1 Corinthians."],[1302.86,"But now they've left, they've gone outside, they've desecrated themselves."],[1307.42,"They're not holy and set apart anymore."],[1309.34,"So they need to come back, and in coming back, they need to purify themselves."],[1313.58,"They need to be cleaned again of the residue of what they've done while they were outside and who they've been so that they can be, so that they can be restored."],[1323.82,"And so this this is. is the place that the confession of sins has in the church as community"],[1332.82,"This is how confession and then absolution work."],[1336.42,"What does St."],[1337.14,"John say in 1 John?"],[1338.42,"If we confess our sins, he's faithful and just to forgive us our sins and cleanse us from all unrighteousness."],[1345.38,"Those aren't just synonyms."],[1346.98,"You got two goats on the Day of Atonement."],[1349.86,"One takes away your sins and the other one cleanses the space."],[1355.22,"And so this is what St."],[1357.22,"Paul's talking about concretely."],[1359.4,"He's not saying go and throw away all your secular CDs."],[1363.24,"I guess that's dating me."],[1364.68,"He's not saying don't go and delete all your uh secular MP3s or."],[1369.04,"Or he's not saying unsubscribe from your secular streaming service, whatever the current um."],[1376.66,"That's not what he's talking about when he's talking about being set apart."],[1379.56,"Only watch the Christian version of things."],[1382.26,"Like that's not where he's going."],[1384.15,"He's saying you need to return, and the way to return and the way to become holy again is to cleanse and purify yourself from all that junk that took you outside in the first place and that's been happening since you've been outside."],[1399.69,"And so this is, and the very, very practical way that's expressed for them and for us, because they had confession and absolution then too."],[1407.45,"Say, James, confess your sins to one another and pray for one another, and you will be healed."],[1412.17,"That's why that healing language is used so much with sin too."],[1412.49,"y It's healing and purifing."],[1412.57,"Something is actually happening"],[1419.17,"It's not just like God's got a list of sins and he erases some of them."],[1426.29,"But what's broken is what you've broken is fixed."],[1429.75,"And what you've tarnished and what you've desecrated in yourself is cleaned and purified and healed."],[1434.81,"So this is happening."],[1435.93,"And this is the very practical way that that happens."],[1439.13,"So he's saying you need to come back and you need to come back and confess."],[1444.21,"ight? R He's remember, he's already told, he's already pushed, and he's going to push again when we pick back up."],[1449.05,"But he's already pushed the people who are still there saying, 'You need to forgive, and you need to be reconciled, and you need to receive these people.' This is the flip side."],[1458.13,"This is you need to come back and you need to confess, and you need to be forgiven, and you need to be purified and cleansed, and you need to be healed."],[1467.97,"So the reason this is very important, that this is how St."],[1472.69,"Paul sees it, coming from the Old Testament background, is that this isn't how we tend to in our contemporary day look at reconciliation."],[1482.37,"We look at reconciliation as, well, the people who are sinned against just need to get over it."],[1488.21,"Or we need to say whatever the dispute was about really wasn't that important."],[1492.05,"So it's all just important that, you know, we're all brothers and sisters in Christ."],[1495.41,"Let's all just get along and just forget about it."],[1498.23,"That's our contemporary version."],[1500.55,"That's not what St."],[1500.95,"St. Paul. is talking about real reconciliation"],[1501.47,"Paul is talking about."],[1506.54,"The people who have been hurt need to be healed."],[1509.26,"The people who have been sinned against need to be willing to forgive the people who sinned against them."],[1513.9,"The people who have sinned need to be willing to confess their sin and admit what they've done and be purified and be healed and be restored and now going forward act differently."],[1525.84,"And maintain this holiness."],[1529.28,"So it's very important for that reason."],[1530.96,"We have this very lazy definition of, you know, can't we all just get along?"],[1534.48,"Where that's the biggest thing."],[1536.5,"Where we try to just sort of paper over problems rather than fixing them."],[1540.58,"And that's not what St."],[1541.62,"Paul is doing."],[1542.42,"St. Paul really wants them to be reconciled and wants them to come back together as a community and to be at peace with one another, but he knows that the only way that happens is to really fix what is broken, to really repair the sin."],[1555.66,"That's why in 1 Corinthians, he didn't just say, Well, yeah, what those people are doing is wrong, but you know, we gotta love them."],[1566.68,"He said, No, if they won't repent and they won't stop, you have to throw them out of the church and turn them over to Satan."],[1573.04,"So that maybe once they're out there in Satan's world, maybe that'll shock them to their senses and they'll come back and repent."],[1580.64,"Because they need to be healed and they need to be fixed and they need to be restored and they need to come to know Christ more deeply and they need to find salvation."],[1587.52,"That's what St."],[1588.16,"Paul wants. Not just a big, happy, growing church with as many people as possible."],[1595.4,"That's not his goal."],[1597.88,"And so this needs to be our goal too, obviously."],[1602.6,"And also we need to hear St."],[1604.6,"Paul's call to be reconciled."],[1607.1,"To be forgiven, to be healed."],[1609.26,"Because it's also very tempting for us when we know we've done wrong."],[1613.58,"Well, I'll just stop doing that and we'll get on with it."],[1616.22,"Or if I've wronged a person, well, they don't seem so upset about it anymore."],[1619.18,"Maybe we can just all pretend nothing happened."],[1621.66,"It's very tempting to us to try not to actually fix the problems, to deal with the symptoms rather than the disease."],[1628.94,"But the symptoms are there to tell you you have the disease, so the disease doesn't. kill you"],[1634.5,"So if we just try to get rid of the symptoms, we're gonna let the disease kill us without even realizing it."],[1642.84,"So uh this is this is what St."],[1644.68,"Paul's getting at."],[1645.48,"So I think you guys had questions, and I ran right over you."],[1648.76,"That answered it."],[1649.88,"Oh, okay. Did that cover the ground?"],[1652.28,"I have a question, and this is something that has been answered before, but I keep losing my grip on it."],[1660.12,"The phrase the fear of God."],[1662.2,"Yes. I mean, we we think of God as loving and forgiving and all of that."],[1668.12,"He's also frighteningly powerful and big and beyond our understanding."],[1677.0,"So what is the fear of God between those two things?"],[1682.97,"What is the fear of God supposed to be?"],[1685.45,"The the fear of God fear here is being used to talk about awe and reverence."],[1690.41,"Okay. Awe, reverence, respect."],[1693.69,"And this this is again a part of fatherhood that we've maybe lost a little bit of in the modern world, where it's again, you know, God's our dad, you know."],[1704.57,"That's not really that's not fatherhood."],[1708.27,"In the sense that the that the scriptures talk right, that's not the potter Familias."],[1712.36,"That's not and you know, I mean, I'm not that old, but I mean, when I was growing up, I knew my dad loved me."],[1720.36,"We could joke around."],[1721.24,"I could have fun with my dad"],[1723.98,"But there was a line, of like where that went into disrespect."],[1728.64,"And I knew if I crossed that line, which happened a few times, that I needed to be a little afraid of my dad."],[1737.02,"Not because he was a terrible person, but because I had crossed a line of respect and you know reverence and that kind of thing."],[1745.98,"And so the fear of God is the same kind of thing."],[1749.44,"And we keep those in balance."],[1750.97,"Like when I come out with the Eucharist to commune people."],[1755.23,"I say, in the fear of God with faith and love draw near."],[1759.07,"Those things are all, those things all go together."],[1762.75,"Those things all go together."],[1764.55,"You can't really separate love and respect."],[1767.37,"You can't c claim you love someone and then have no respect for them and no reverence for them."],[1773.19,"I mean you could say it, but you know it's not true."],[1777.17,"So that's that's that that awe and reverence."],[1781.89,"Last Sunday I remember during uh Orthros, God was described as terrible."],[1787.57,"Yeah. That was the old definition of terrible."],[1791.97,"Like Ivan the Terrible."],[1794.53,"Like, causes terror."],[1796.37,"Yeah. Not like terrible, like, lousy."],[1799.09,"Yeah. Like the way we used to say."],[1801.25,"Like fighting. Yeah."],[1802.45,"Uh-hhu. It's like when they say that like the fear of the Lord is the beginning of the wisdom."],[1807.09,"Yeah So it's like awe and reverence of the Lord."],[1809.69,"Well, what is it?"],[1810.89,"And that's also related to the fool says in his heart there is no God."],[1814.81,"Like we like to throw that at atheists."],[1816.49,"There weren't any atheists in the ancient world."],[1819.69,"Okay. There was nobody walking around not believing in the suit."],[1822.41,"Well, I should say nobody."],[1823.37,"There were some cynics."],[1824.65,"But who didn't believe in anything?"],[1826.97,"But for the most part."],[1828.98,"99.999 repeating percent of people in the ancient world believe that there was some kind of supernatural something that you would call a god or gods or spirits or something."],[1839.99,"The fool says in the heart there is no God is talking about not having that fear or that reverence."],[1846.71,"That you just go and do what you want as if God wasn't there, as if he wasn't didn't know what you were doing, as if there was gonna be no justice and no consequences."],[1859.01,"So that's talking about that same thing, not having that kind of awe and reverence for who for who God is."],[1865.73,"So that could apply to I guess what you would call lukewarm Christians, uh atheists alike."],[1872.29,"We'll get to that in revelation with Luke Walm Christians."],[1874.37,"But yeah, yeah, that can apply to anyone."],[1876.53,"In fact, that there are atheists who act as if they're inconsistent and are actually very moral people, even though you know."],[1885.39,"Mark Twain once said it uh an atheist is a person who is good for nothing."],[1890.27,"Meaning that they're probably a good person, but for no particular reason."],[1895.49,"They're not gonna get rewarded for it."],[1897.17,"They're not gonna get right, there's no threat of punishment."],[1899.73,"They just you know so but yeah, that and that's I mean St."],[1904.93,"Paul talks about that in Romans too about the Gentiles."],[1907.18,"He says the Gentiles didn't get the law, but a lot of them keep it better than a lot of Jewish people in terms of their behavior."],[1913.58,"You know, in terms of but yeah, so that's anyone who just sort of doesn't have that awe and respect for they may say, Oh yeah, well, yeah, there's a God, but they don't that doesn't affect right how they live."],[1926.38,"But yeah, that's what it's that's what it's getting at."],[1929.28,"And remember, there's that therefore."],[1931.28,"So we do that the fear of God because he's talking about God being our father."],[1935.6,"At the beginning he says, because we have these promises, those are the promises."],[1938.96,"He'll be our God, we'll be his people."],[1939.04,"'ll be our In the first quote, he father, we'll be his sons and daughters."],[1946.36,"That those are the promises."],[1948.44,"And so we do that with reverence that that's the flip side of him being our father is us having that respect and awe and reverence for him as our father."],[1958.76,"So I'm just seeing if we set another record as we move to verse two of 2 Corinthians chapter 7."],[1970.28,"Open your hearts to us."],[1971.8,"We have wronged no one, we have corrupted no one, we have cheated no one."],[1976.36,"I do not say this to condemn, for I've said before that you are in our hearts to die together and to live together."],[1983.88,"Great isat is my boldness of speech toward you, gre my boasting on your behalf."],[1988.56,"I am filled with comfort, I am exceedingly joyful in all our tribulation."],[1993.84,"So this is again, I mean, St."],[1996.16,"Paul is basically pleading with these people to come back into the church."],[2000.8,"Now notice he's not pleading with them out of desperation, like we'll take you back no matter what."],[2005.86,"He's just said what they need to do to come back, but he really wants them to come back and repent and be reconciled."],[2013.96,"And to find salvation."],[2015.64,"So you don't have to sort of lower the bar."],[2018.62,"You don't have to wash out your Christianity in order to have a desire to see people come to Christ."],[2025.1,"So you don't have to sell a coffee bar or something?"],[2027.1,"a Or band."],[2027.47,"Yeah. So and so he's saying, he says, look, you know, we haven't done anything to wrong you, we haven't cheated you, we haven't."],[2039.18,"That we being him, but he and right, Reverend St."],[2041.42,"Timothy is his co-author, but all of the people with St."],[2044.54,"Paul, he's like, and the and Apollos has come to them, saying, We didn't do any of this to you."],[2049.84,"So you don't have a reason to have a grudge against us."],[2053.06,"Who are now asking you to come and be reconciled."],[2055.22,"You may have some grudges against the other people in the church at Corinth."],[2058.38,"You may have some issues with them."],[2060.06,"He says, but St."],[2061.02,"Paul's saying, you have no reason to have any issue with me."],[2063.58,"So hear what I'm saying."],[2065.5,"I'm asking you."],[2067.42,"I'm calling you to do this, I'm encouraging you to do this."],[2070.3,"I'm curious about the history"],[2070.74,"I haven't done anything to wrong you, so please hear me."],[2076.5,"I don't understand the history of this."],[2078.9,"Would these people have been kind of in awe of him already?"],[2083.86,"I was of St."],[2085.3,"Paul? Yeah. Yeah, I don't think they're in awe and but they have a relationship with him."],[2090.34,"They know him."],[2090.98,"He spent years with them."],[2092.9,"And what that means is, we talked about this a little in the book of Acts when it talked about like him spending years in Corinth."],[2099.4,"It doesn't just mean, oh, he lived there."],[2102.04,"He was there preaching to them."],[2104.14,"Every Sabbath. He was there giving them the Eucharist."],[2107.58,"But he was there on like traveling around and performing miracles."],[2112.46,"Yeah, but he had spent several years just there in Corinth."],[2114.38,"He was at their funerals"],[2116.34,"He was at their weddings."],[2120.18,"He was at their he was baptizing people."],[2122.9,"He was right."],[2124.1,"I mean, he was there doing all those."],[2126.58,"They basically had him for their priest for a few years."],[2129.64,"In our modern terminology."],[2131.88,"Really more like as their bishop."],[2133.5,"But but right, they're doing all these things and being a part of their lives."],[2138.55,"And he was, and he said in 1 Corinthians, he said, you know, you've had a lot of teachers."],[2143.03,"You've had a whole bunch of people come there and teach you, but you've only had one father, that being Saint Paul."],[2148.65,"He said, I was like a father, you, you're like my children in the gospel."],[2151.93,"So he was a part of Familius, too."],[2155.03,"In a sense."],[2155.83,"Yeah, in a sense."],[2156.79,"So they would have had some awe."],[2157.59,"And so he's saying, look, you know me"],[2159.79,"They have respect for sure."],[2161.23,"Respect. Yeah. You know me."],[2165.47,"You know how I've always been toward you."],[2167.71,"So some of those people of the church might ask you to come back, encourage you to come back, and you might be suspicious of them and their motives and everything."],[2174.85,"Because of all the water that's gone under the bridge."],[2177.41,"He says, but you know who I am."],[2179.73,"I'm asking you."],[2181.43,"I'm the one encouraging you to do this."],[2183.75,"Because I want to see you, you know, come to salvation."],[2187.68,"So they they had this idea, maybe he was just coming in like a rock star."],[2194.88,"Yes, I think I always had that idea before, because I I never processed the idea that he was there for years."],[2201.52,"Yeah I always visualize him as going from city to city to city to city, not thinking about how long he spent in each one."],[2213.0,"There are different situations."],[2214.2,"Like when we were going through Romans, St."],[2217.32,"Paul wrote his epistle to the Romans before he had ever been there."],[2220.2,"So he had a different sort of relationship there."],[2222.92,"To them, he's writing as this apostle with this reputation."],[2226.86,"And so he's writing based on that reputation."],[2229.82,"But with the Corinthians, he spent time there and they know him."],[2234.86,"So it would be like Romans would be like somebody reading my blog."],[2240.8,"Who's never met me."],[2242.32,"But they're just reading the blog, and they're like, oh, he seems to know what he's talking about. so they give it whatever weight they give it"],[2249.16,"Where second Corinthians would be like when I preach here at the church, where people have gotten to know me, you know, for years, and that kind of thing."],[2258.04,"And so it's different."],[2259.58,"When somebody who you know and who you know, hopefully everybody here knows I care about them."],[2264.56,"And and who you've you've shared things with."],[2267.29,"When when they tell you something, it's different than it when it's just, you know, some clergyman on the internet."],[2274.99,"And that's that's what St."],[2276.19,"Paul's appealing to here is that personal relationship he has with them."],[2280.11,"Kind of reinforce what he's saying."],[2282.35,"And the end of that in verse 4 was talking about his lever."],[2286.83,"He says, Great is my boldness of speech toward you."],[2288.75,"right Sort of permission to speak freely?"],[2290.69,"I'm talking to you this way because we know each other."],[2293.75,"So I'm just being open and honest with you."],[2296.17,"I'm not mincing words or flattering you or using fancy arguments."],[2301.06,"Like we know each other."],[2302.74,"And the great is my boasting on your behalf."],[2305.72,"He's saying he's proud of them."],[2308.44,"He's proud of the church in Corinth."],[2311.32,"When he goes to other churches, he talks about the church in Corinth and the time he spent there."],[2317.9,"That goes into that him being a father to them."],[2320.7,"He's proud of his kids in Corinth."],[2324.99,"He says he's filled with comfort, and he's exceedingly joyful in all their tribulation."],[2329.17,"He's saying, and they're saying that because of them."],[2332.14,"He says, Because I know what can happen here."],[2335.44,"Things have been bad."],[2336.66,"But because I know what can happen here, even in all the troubles St."],[2340.02,"Paul is having, where he is as he writes this, he's still joyful."],[2345.86,"Is there any idea how big the church was at this point?"],[2348.9,"How many people have we don't have a good estimate?"],[2353.7,"I mean, it wouldn't have been more than a couple hundred people at the outside at this point."],[2359.3,"And it's kind of fractured at at this particular point to boot."],[2363.24,"So yeah, it's I mean, this is a small group now."],[2367.48,"So as we've seen, it's mostly Gentile converts."],[2371.88,"So it's probably smaller than the Jewish community."],[2376.32,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2383.68,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecouncil at ancient faith dot com."],[2389.04,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com"]],"o":[{"t":22.72,"lv":2,"h":"Chapter breaks aren't Paul's","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>2 Corinthians 7</b> - but <b>not much of a break</b> in the text</li><li class=\"d0\">Chapter and verse breaks added <b>many centuries later</b></li><li class=\"d1\">chapters often based on the <b>lectionary</b> - chunks of a set length</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes at a real theme break, sometimes just \"gone a long time since we had one\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Verses: English makes them look like <b>one per sentence</b> - <b>doesn't match Greek</b></li><li class=\"d1\">several Greek sentences in one English verse, and vice versa</li><li class=\"d1\">Greek sentences long and complex → broken into <b>4-5 English sentences</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So important tonight: need to be <b>caught up</b> before reading</li></ul>"},{"t":104.92,"lv":3,"h":"Greek sentences and punctuation","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Greek not as bad as <b>Latin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Caesar's <i>Gaul</i>: one sentence runs <b>17 pages</b>, subject is the <b>last word</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Paul</b>: relatively <b>sophisticated Greek</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sentence can run a <b>page or two</b> - no run-ons, just keep adding clauses</li><li class=\"d0\">Originally <b>all capitals, no punctuation, no spaces, no hyphens</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even sentence breaks in a printed Greek NT were put in <b>by someone later</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we don't actually know for sure when his sentences end\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Early punctuators were <b>native Greek speakers</b> - better idea than us, but \"wouldn't stake my life and reputation on it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":208.01,"lv":2,"h":"Example: the voice crying in the wilderness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Isaiah</b> quote in the <b>Synoptics</b> (not sure John has it)</li><li class=\"d1\">English since the KJV: \"a voice crying out in the wilderness, <b>comma</b>, make straight the way of the Lord\"</li><li class=\"d1\">no punctuation in Greek: could be \"a voice crying out, <b>comma</b>, in the wilderness make straight the way\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Which it is = \"a completely <b>subjective judgment</b>\" - no clue in the text</li><li class=\"d0\">His view: <b>second one</b>, based on <b>context in Isaiah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he holds NT authors quote the OT <b>in context</b></li><li class=\"d1\">others: NT authors take OT verses and <b>do something different</b> with them → OT context doesn't apply → no way to know</li><li class=\"d0\">Only thing that could decide it: whether OT context matters</li></ul>"},{"t":327.88,"lv":3,"h":"Why repeat this","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">A bit off topic, but worth repeating</li><li class=\"d0\">We read chapters like <b>chapters in a book</b> - author designed it that way</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not how Paul wrote</b> - no chapters, no sections</li><li class=\"d1\">someone's judgment, often <b>liturgical</b> not textual/interpretive reasons</li><li class=\"d0\">Even <b>quotation marks</b> and where they go are debated</li></ul>"},{"t":386.9,"lv":2,"h":"Where we left off: come back to the community","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul just finished a series of <b>texts about holiness</b> = being <b>separated</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Goal of <b>2 Corinthians</b>: bring the <b>Corinthian community back together</b>, reconciled</li><li class=\"d1\">after the problems of <b>1 Corinthians</b>, his visit, then a <b>split</b></li><li class=\"d1\">some <b>disciplined / put out</b> - Paul wants them <b>reconciled and reincorporated</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Last time speaking to those <b>alienated or excommunicated</b> (by the church, or just felt that way)</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>No man is an island</b>\" - outside this community you'll join <b>some other</b></li><li class=\"d1\">pagan city community, <b>trade guild</b> worshiping other gods</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: you're <b>baptized, put on Christ, received the Spirit</b> - a different person</li><li class=\"d1\">can't have <b>fellowship</b> with those communities anymore</li><li class=\"d0\">So <b>come back and be restored</b></li><li class=\"d1\">this is where <b>Christ dwells</b>, <b>salvation</b> happens, the <b>holy community</b></li><li class=\"d1\">requires <b>humility and forgiveness on all sides</b> - \"an imperative\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Last quote = <b>two quotes put together</b>: \"come out from among them and be separate... I will be a father to you\"</li></ul>"},{"t":594.4,"lv":2,"h":"Family imagery and the paterfamilias","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Family language: Acts calls Christians in a city <b>the brethren</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just Christian - <b>1st-c. community language</b>, whole society on family models</li><li class=\"d0\">Roman <i>pater familias</i> = head of the extended family (\"you sound smarter in Latin\")</li><li class=\"d1\">reduplicated in <b>cities</b> (including the city's gods) and the <b>empire</b> - emperor = father of the empire</li><li class=\"d0\">Why Paul moves easily between <b>community and family</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just which community - <b>which family</b>; join the church → <b>God as father</b>; join another → someone you don't want</li><li class=\"d0\">Paterfamilias had <b>power of life and death</b></li><li class=\"d1\">baby brought to him to <b>inspect for defects</b>, decide to keep or <b>abandon</b></li><li class=\"d1\">master of slaves; could <b>execute</b> or <b>sell</b> family members</li></ul>"},{"t":730.75,"lv":3,"h":"Choosing your father","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul's offer: be in <b>God's family</b>, knowing his character through <b>Christ</b>, with that power over your life</li><li class=\"d1\">or join another family with a <b>pagan god or Caesar</b> having that power</li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"wouldn't it be nice to have God as dad\"</li><li class=\"d1\">who has <b>power of life and death</b>, is your <b>benefactor</b>, shapes your destiny?</li><li class=\"d1\">any sane person in the ancient world would choose <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pagan gods not viewed that way: \"nobody's saying <b>what a friend we have in Zeus</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Zeus: worry he'd come make love to you by force - <b>nobody thought Zeus loved them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">gods <b>appeased, placated, manipulated</b> - no relationship</li><li class=\"d0\">Liturgy: God is <b>good and loves mankind</b> - a <b>bizarre notion</b> then; we pass over it now</li><li class=\"d0\">Argument is \"incredibly strong\": creator who <b>loves you, wants to be your father</b> - or \"back out there into the darkness\"</li></ul>"},{"t":893.57,"lv":2,"h":"2 Corinthians 7:1 - Perfecting holiness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:1</b>: \"Therefore, having these promises, beloved, let us <b>cleanse ourselves</b> from all filthiness of flesh and spirit, <b>perfecting holiness in the fear of God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Therefore\"</b> = Paul's transition: I've explained; <b>here's how it applies</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Sounds like repeating himself - but <b>holiness and purification are not synonyms</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Holiness = set apart</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"perfect holiness\" = <b>truly separate</b> from the world and flesh, from what ensnared you before</li><li class=\"d0\">How? Concept <b>foreign to us</b>, not to people who knew the <b>OT</b></li><li class=\"d1\">about <b>sacred space, sacred things, sacred people</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1018.1,"lv":2,"h":"Holy things: the chalice and the Khan cup","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Setting something apart makes it holy</li><li class=\"d1\">church <b>chalice</b> vs. his <b>Star Trek II: Wrath of Khan</b> collector's cup</li><li class=\"d1\">not holy because of <b>magical metal from a holy mountain</b>, gems from Mount Athos</li><li class=\"d1\">holy because of <b>what it's used for</b> - only the body and blood of Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">Khan cup not evil - just a <b>common thing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Holy things can be <b>desecrated</b></li><li class=\"d1\">break in, drink <b>whiskey</b> from the chalice, throw it around → <b>no longer holy</b>, used for common things</li><li class=\"d0\">Apostles' view of <b>our holiness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in <b>baptism and the Spirit</b> we are <b>set apart</b> for holy purposes</li><li class=\"d1\">1 Cor: \"your body is the <b>temple of the Holy Spirit</b>\" - can't pollute it</li><li class=\"d1\">use body, mind, soul for common things → <b>desecrated</b>, no longer set apart</li></ul>"},{"t":1159.23,"lv":3,"h":"Cleansing and the Day of Atonement","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Desecrated chalice: must be <b>reconsecrated</b> - cleanse, wash, repair, <b>set apart again</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same for <b>space</b>: <b>Day of Atonement</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>two goats</b>: one carries the people's sins into the wilderness</li><li class=\"d1\">other, <b>for Yahweh</b>, sacrificed; blood used to <b>make atonement for physical objects</b> in the Holy of Holies - altar, ark</li><li class=\"d1\">camp and temple activity has <b>desecrated</b> them → purified so God can keep <b>dwelling</b> there</li><li class=\"d0\">Why a yearly day when there are <b>sin offerings</b> all the time?</li><li class=\"d1\">to <b>purify the sacred space and things</b> from what's been done with them</li></ul>"},{"t":1277.93,"lv":2,"h":"Applied to the Corinthians: confession","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul applies it to the Corinthians, then us</li><li class=\"d1\">they were <b>baptized, received the Spirit and Eucharist</b>, called <b>saints / holy ones</b> in 1 Cor</li><li class=\"d1\">then <b>left, desecrated themselves</b> → need to <b>come back and be purified</b> of the residue</li><li class=\"d0\">This is the place of <b>confession and absolution</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>1 John</b>: \"faithful and just to <b>forgive</b> our sins and <b>cleanse</b> us from all unrighteousness\" - not synonyms</li><li class=\"d1\">= the two goats: one <b>takes away sins</b>, other <b>cleanses the space</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Not</b> \"throw out your secular CDs / delete MP3s / unsubscribe from streaming / only watch Christian versions\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Return</b> and be cleansed of the junk that took you out</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>James</b>: \"confess your sins to one another... you will be <b>healed</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">healing and purifying - <b>something actually happens</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not God erasing items off a list: what's <b>broken is fixed</b>, what's tarnished <b>cleaned and healed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Flip side of his push to the others to <b>forgive and receive</b> them back</li></ul>"},{"t":1467.97,"lv":3,"h":"Real reconciliation, not \"can't we all get along\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Contemporary view: hurt people <b>get over it</b>, dispute \"wasn't important\", \"let's all just get along\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's real reconciliation</li><li class=\"d1\">hurt people <b>healed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sinned-against <b>willing to forgive</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sinners <b>confess, admit, be purified, healed, restored</b>, then <b>act differently</b> and maintain holiness</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Lazy definition\" - <b>papering over problems</b> rather than fixing them</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul wants peace but knows it needs <b>real repair of the sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">1 Cor: not \"we gotta love them\" - <b>throw them out and turn them over to Satan</b> so they come to their senses and repent</li><li class=\"d1\">goal = <b>healing, restoration, knowing Christ, salvation</b> - \"not just a big, happy, growing church\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Our temptation too: stop doing it, or \"they don't seem upset anymore, pretend nothing happened\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>treating symptoms, not the disease</b> - symptoms are there so the disease doesn't kill you</li></ul>"},{"t":1645.48,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: the fear of God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: \"Fear of God\" - God is loving and forgiving but also <b>frighteningly powerful</b>; what is it?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: <b>awe, reverence, respect</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Part of <b>fatherhood</b> we've lost - \"God's our dad\" isn't the <i>pater familias</i></li><li class=\"d1\">his own dad: could joke around, but <b>a line</b> where it became disrespect; crossing it, \"needed to be a little afraid\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not because he was terrible - crossed a line of <b>respect and reverence</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Kept in balance: at Communion, \"<b>in the fear of God, with faith and love draw near</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can't claim love with <b>no respect or reverence</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1781.89,"lv":3,"h":"\"Terrible\" and the fool who says there is no God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Orthros: God called <b>\"terrible\"</b> = old sense, like <b>Ivan the Terrible</b> - causes terror, not \"lousy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom</b>\" = awe and reverence</li><li class=\"d0\">\"The <b>fool says in his heart there is no God</b>\" - we throw it at atheists</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>no atheists in the ancient world</b> (some cynics) - <b>99.999%</b> believed in gods or spirits</li><li class=\"d1\">it means <b>no fear or reverence</b>: acting as if God didn't know, no <b>justice or consequences</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Applies to <b>lukewarm Christians</b> and atheists alike (\"we'll get to that in Revelation\")</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mark Twain</b>: atheist is \"good for nothing\" - good, but for no particular reason; no reward, no punishment</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans</b>: Gentiles without the law often keep it <b>better than Jews</b> in behavior</li><li class=\"d0\">Anyone who says \"there's a God\" but it doesn't affect how they live</li><li class=\"d0\">Back to <b>\"therefore\"</b>: fear of God goes with the <b>promises</b> - he's our <b>father</b>; reverence is the flip side</li></ul>"},{"t":1958.76,"lv":2,"h":"2 Corinthians 7:2-4 - Open your hearts","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>7:2-4</b>: \"Open your hearts to us. We have <b>wronged no one, corrupted no one, cheated no one</b>... you are in our hearts, to die together and to live together... great is my <b>boldness of speech</b> toward you, great my <b>boasting</b> on your behalf... filled with comfort, exceedingly joyful in all our tribulation\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul is <b>pleading</b> with them to come back</li><li class=\"d1\">not out of desperation: \"we'll take you back no matter what\" - he just said <b>what they need to do</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>don't lower the bar</b> or wash out Christianity to want people to come to Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">joke: \"you don't have to sell a <b>coffee bar</b> or a band\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"We\" = Paul, <b>Timothy</b> co-author, everyone with him, <b>Apollos</b> too</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we didn't do any of this to you\" → <b>no grudge</b> against us</li><li class=\"d1\">may have grudges against <b>others in Corinth</b>, not against Paul</li><li class=\"d1\">so <b>hear me</b> - I'm calling you to do this</li></ul>"},{"t":2070.74,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: Paul's history with them","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Would they have been <b>in awe</b> of him already?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: Not awe, but <b>respect and relationship</b> - he <b>spent years</b> there</li><li class=\"d1\">not just \"lived there\" - preaching every <b>Sabbath</b>, giving the <b>Eucharist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not traveling and performing miracles: at their <b>funerals, weddings</b>, baptizing</li><li class=\"d1\">\"basically had him for their priest\" - really <b>more like bishop</b></li><li class=\"d0\">1 Cor: <b>many teachers, only one father</b> - \"my children in the gospel\"</li><li class=\"d1\">so he was part of their <i>familia</i> too</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: \"<b>you know me</b>, how I've been toward you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">church members asking them back may be <b>suspected</b> - \"all the water under the bridge\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but \"I'm the one encouraging you\" - want you to come to <b>salvation</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2187.68,"lv":3,"h":"Corinth vs. Romans: knowing the writer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Questioner pictured Paul as a <b>rock star</b>, city to city - never thought of <b>how long he stayed</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans</b>: written <b>before he'd ever been there</b> - apostle writing on his <b>reputation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like someone reading <b>my blog</b> who's never met me</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2 Corinthians</b> = like preaching at <b>my own church</b>, people who've known me for <b>years</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what someone you know cares about you says carries <b>different weight</b> than \"some clergyman on the internet\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul appeals to that <b>personal relationship</b> to reinforce what he says</li></ul>"},{"t":2282.35,"lv":2,"h":"7:4 - Boldness, boasting, joy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Great is my boldness of speech\"</b> = permission to speak freely</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we know each other\" → open and honest, <b>not mincing words, flattering, or fancy arguments</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Great is my boasting on your behalf\"</b> = he's <b>proud</b> of them</li><li class=\"d1\">tells other churches about Corinth and his time there; \"proud of his <b>kids</b> in Corinth\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Filled with comfort, exceedingly joyful</b> in all their tribulation</li><li class=\"d1\">things have been bad, but he knows <b>what can happen here</b></li><li class=\"d1\">joyful even in his own troubles as he writes</li></ul>"},{"t":2345.86,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: size of the Corinthian church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: How big was the church?</li><li class=\"d0\">A: No good estimate - no more than <b>a couple hundred</b> at the outside</li><li class=\"d1\">and <b>fractured</b> at this point - \"a small group now\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mostly Gentile converts</b> → probably smaller than the <b>Jewish community</b></li></ul>"}]});
